latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Input Grid:\n   a. Examine the entire 24x24 grid, with special focus on the central 12x12 area.\n   b. Create a color frequency map for the entire grid and the central area.\n   c. Identify color transitions and relationships (adjacent, diagonal) in the central area.\n\n2. Identify Key Colors:\n   a. Select 4-6 colors that are most significant in defining the pattern's essence.\n   b. Consider both frequency and the role of colors in forming the pattern.\n   c. Include any accent colors that play a crucial role in the overall design.\n\n3. Determine Color Relationships:\n   a. Analyze how the key colors interact with each other in the input grid.\n   b. Note common color pairs, transitions, and their relative positions.\n   c. Identify any dominant directional patterns (horizontal, vertical, diagonal).\n\n4. Assess Pattern Complexity:\n   a. Evaluate the complexity of color interactions and transitions.\n   b. Consider the number of key colors and their relationship patterns.\n\n5. Determine Output Grid Size:\n   a. Based on the pattern complexity, choose a size between 3x3 and 8x8.\n   b. Ensure the chosen size can adequately represent the key color relationships.\n\n6. Create Initial Output Grid:\n   a. Start with a grid of the determined size, filled with the most dominant color.\n   b. Place the second most dominant color in a pattern reflecting its role in the input.\n\n7. Implement Color Relationships:\n   a. Add the remaining key colors to the grid.\n   b. Arrange colors to reflect the relationships observed in the input.\n   c. If diagonal patterns were significant in the input, incorporate them.\n\n8. Refine the Pattern:\n   a. Adjust color placements to best capture the essence of the input pattern.\n   b. Ensure that color transitions in the output reflect those in the input.\n   c. If applicable, incorporate any significant accent colors.\n\n9. Balance Color Distribution:\n   a. Compare the color distribution in the output to that of the input's central area.\n   b. Make minor adjustments to better match the input's color balance if necessary.\n\n10. Validate and Iterate:\n    a. Check if the output captures the essence of the input pattern.\n    b. Verify that all key colors are represented appropriately.\n    c. If the result doesn't feel right, iterate from step 6 or 7, trying a different arrangement.\n\n11. Final Refinement:\n    a. Look for any opportunities to simplify the pattern without losing its essence.\n    b. Ensure the output grid is visually balanced and representative of the input.\n\n12. Return Result:\n    a. Convert the final grid into the required ColoredGrid format.\n    b. Return the ColoredGrid object as the solution.\n\nThis plan is flexible enough to handle the variations we've seen in the examples, focusing on capturing the essence of the input pattern rather than a direct translation. It allows for different output sizes and pattern complexities, which is crucial given the diversity in the example outputs."
